import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap

## The crypto package: installing it, a signed link as an app writes one, the modules, what they
## are checked against, and what isn't here. Static: the sample is server/test/app/Samples.roc's,
## which the server's tests run.
meta = {
	title: "Crypto",
	description: "The crypto package for Roc Solid apps: SHA-256, HMAC-SHA256, HKDF-SHA256, Base64 and hex, in plain Roc on no platform, each checked against its RFC's test vectors.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "install", label: "Installing it" },
		{ id: "links", label: "A signed link" },
		{ id: "modules", label: "The modules" },
		{ id: "checked", label: "How it is tested" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "What isn't here" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/crypto</code>]}
				title="Hashes, signatures and keys, in Roc"
			>
				SHA-256, HMAC, HKDF, Base64 and hex. You use them to store a token as its digest, sign a link so
				nobody can forge it, and derive separate keys from one secret. The package is plain Roc on no
				platform, so the server, a page in the browser and an <code>expect</code> all run the same code.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="install">Installing it</Heading>
			<p>Name the package in your app's header, beside the platform:</p>
			<Code
				title="main.roc's header"
				source={"app [program] { crypto: \"../packages/crypto/main.roc\", pf: platform \"../server/platform/main.roc\" }"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				Then import the modules where you use them: <code>import crypto.Hmac</code>,
				<code>import crypto.Base64</code>, and so on. Encryption and random bytes need the operating
				system, so they are the server platform's, not this package's: <code>AesGcm</code> and
				<code>Random</code> (<a href={Paths.platform_crypto}>the platform's crypto page</a> shows them with
				this package, for tokens and sealed secrets).
			</p>

			<Heading id="links">A signed link</Heading>
			<p>
				A link that carries who it is for and when it expires, signed so that changing either breaks it.
				This is from the server platform's test app:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_links.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_links.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				The key that signs links comes from the app's <code>APP_SECRET</code> through HKDF, with
				<code>"links"</code> as its purpose, so this key signs links and nothing else. The link's text is
				signed with HMAC-SHA256, and the signature is written in Base64's URL alphabet, which needs no
				escaping in a URL. <code>Hmac.verify_sha256</code> compares signatures in constant time: how long
				it takes doesn't tell a forger how much of a signature was right.
			</p>

			<Heading id="modules">The modules</Heading>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>What it has</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Sha256</code></td><td><code>hash</code> (the 32-byte digest), <code>hex</code> (the digest as 64 lowercase hex digits), <code>chunks</code> (the digest of several pieces, without joining them first)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Hmac</code></td><td><code>sha256(key, message)</code>; <code>verify_sha256(key, message, signature)</code>; <code>equal</code>, a constant-time comparison of two byte lists</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Hkdf</code></td><td><code>sha256(key, salt, info, length)</code>, which fails with <code>TooLong</code> past 8160 bytes; its two steps, <code>extract</code> and <code>expand</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Base64</code></td><td><code>encode</code> and <code>decode</code> (standard alphabet, padded); <code>url_encode</code> (URL alphabet, unpadded) and <code>url_decode</code>; <code>encode_unpadded</code>, <code>url_encode_padded</code>; <code>lines</code> (76-character lines, as MIME wants)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Hex</code></td><td><code>encode</code> (lowercase), <code>decode</code> (either case)</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				Decoding is strict, so each value has exactly one text: only the alphabet asked for, no spaces or
				line breaks, padding either left out or exactly right, and the unused bits of the last character
				zero. Anything else is an error (<code>InvalidBase64</code>, <code>InvalidHex</code>). The
				package's <code>README.md</code> (<code>packages/crypto/</code>) lists every function's type.
			</p>

			<Heading id="checked">How it is tested</Heading>
			<p>
				Each module's <code>expect</code>s check it against published test vectors: NIST's SHA-256
				examples, every test case of RFC 4231 (HMAC), RFC 5869's SHA-256 cases (HKDF), and RFC 4648's
				(Base64 and hex). <code>Sha256</code> wraps Roc's builtin <code>Crypto.SHA256</code>. The server
				platform's tests also compare each module with Node's <code>crypto</code>, through a running
				server.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">What isn't here</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>Encryption and random bytes: they are the server platform's <code>AesGcm</code> and <code>Random</code>.</li>
				<li>Password hashing (bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2): not yet. SHA-256 and HKDF are too fast to protect passwords.</li>
				<li>
					Public-key cryptography: the server platform's <code>P256</code> has as much as Web Push needs
					(ES256 signatures, and ECDH with a key pair made for one call). Verifying a signature, other
					curves and RSA: not yet.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
